            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Diatomaceous earth is the strongest common pick, because its extreme porosity lets it hold a lot of oil. Clay kitty litter ground fine and powdered whiting cost less and also work well, while flour or cornstarch will do in a pinch. Blend the powder with a degreaser or solvent until it forms a thick paste that holds its place without running."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long should an enzyme cleaner stay on concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The label has the final word, but most products need a day or more without rinsing and keep working for a week or longer while the area stays damp. Cold slows the microbes, so expect faster results once the concrete is above roughly 50 degrees Fahrenheit. Keep bleach and acid away from the area while the treatment works."
          }
        },
