          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only for a while. Coatings bond poorly to concrete contaminated with oil and commonly bubble or lift over those spots. Coating makers specify a clean, degreased surface before you etch or grind. Keep treating each stain until water soaks into it evenly, then prepare the whole floor exactly as the coating instructions describe."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a hot water pressure washer worth renting?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Often, for a whole garage floor or a badly stained shop slab. Heated water thins oil so it lets go of the pores much more readily than it does under cold water. At the time of writing, rental shops typically charge roughly $100 to $200 a day. If you only have one or two spots, a poultice costs less and works just as well."
          }
