          Park a leaking car on a garage floor for five years and the oil ends up a quarter inch or more inside the slab. A brush and some degreaser clean the top layer, the floor looks better for a day, and then the mark creeps back as deeper oil migrates upward. The fix is to work with the way concrete behaves: draw the oil out with a poultice, let microbes digest it, or thin it with heat, and give whichever method you choose enough time. Below, all three are applied to garage floors, basement slabs, shop floors and old driveway stains.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How to Get Oil Out of Concrete: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most set-in stains, scrub the surface with degreaser, then lay a diatomaceous earth poultice under plastic for 24 to 48 hours and repeat it until the stain stops fading. Stains that are years old and have spread into a faded halo respond better to an enzyme cleaner kept damp for one to three weeks while the concrete is above roughly 50 degrees Fahrenheit. Fresh spills and blacktop are a different, faster job, covered in our <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">driveway oil stain guide</a>.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li><strong>Degrease the surface.</strong> Tools: broom, strong alkaline degreaser, stiff brush, hose. Time: one scrub, then a full day for the slab to dry before you judge it.</li>
            <li><strong>Check for a sealer.</strong> Tools: a few drops of water. Time: under a minute. Beading means a sealer or coating to strip first; darkening within a minute means bare concrete.</li>
            <li><strong>Mix the poultice.</strong> Tools: diatomaceous earth, water-based degreaser, a bucket. Stir to the thickness of peanut butter.</li>
            <li><strong>Apply and cover it.</strong> Tools: putty knife, plastic sheeting, tape. Spread it a quarter inch thick and an inch past the stain edge, tape the plastic down and poke a few holes. Time: 24 to 48 hours.</li>
            <li><strong>Scrape, rinse and judge it dry.</strong> Tools: plastic putty knife, hose. Time: a day of drying per round. Three to five rounds is normal for an old garage stain.</li>
            <li><strong>Switch to enzymes for old halos.</strong> Tools: enzyme cleaner, stiff brush, plastic or a mister. Time: one to three weeks, kept damp and unrinsed for as long as the label says.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div id="why-it-stays" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Does the Oil Stain Come Back After You Scrub It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Solid as it looks, concrete is riddled with capillary pores, the tiny channels left behind as water evaporated out of the mix while it cured. Oil spreads through those channels by capillary action, the same way coffee climbs up a paper towel, traveling downward and outward at once. The dark halo around an old stain is oil that has crept sideways beneath the surface. A smooth, troweled garage floor slows that travel, while a worn slab that has lost its top cream layer lets the oil move faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is why scrubbing an old stain is so disappointing. Degreaser emulsifies only the oil it reaches, and rinsing carries that portion away, but most of the stain lies deeper than a cleaner can penetrate during a 10-minute dwell. As the slab dries, oil from below wicks toward the drier surface and the mark returns, sometimes almost as dark as it started. Homeowners say the stain came back. In truth it never went anywhere.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What do the methods that work have in common?</h3>

          <p>
            Each one deals with depth. A poultice draws oil upward into an absorbent layer over many hours. Enzyme and microbial cleaners convert hydrocarbons into carbon dioxide and water right where they sit, so nothing has to come to the surface. Hot water and steam thin heavy oil so that a cleaner can flush it out more easily. All three are slower than a quick degreaser rinse, and all three reward extra time far more than extra scrubbing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How clean can an old slab actually get?</h3>

          <p>
            Set your expectations honestly: some very old stains never vanish entirely. A slab that has held oil for a decade may keep a faint shadow even after a thorough job. For those, the realistic target is a spot that blends into the surrounding floor and no longer feels slick or tracks onto shoes. A fresh spill on a driveway is a quicker and different job; our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing oil stains from asphalt and concrete driveways</a> handles that one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="assess" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Can You Tell How Deep the Oil Stain Goes?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean it, let it dry for a day, and see what is left: anything still dark after a good degreaser scrub is inside the slab. That one test, plus two quick checks below, tells you which method to reach for and how long the job is likely to take.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clean the surface and wait a day</h3>

          <p>
            Start by clearing the surface so you can see the real stain. Sweep, scrub the spot with a strong alkaline degreaser, and rinse. That lifts dirt and the surface film, and it shows how much of the mark sits on top compared with how much lives inside the concrete. Wait a full day for the slab to dry before deciding anything. If the stain has mostly disappeared, you are finished. If a dark patch remains, the oil is below the surface and calls for one of the deeper methods.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is the concrete sealed?</h3>

          <p>
            Next, find out whether the slab has a sealer. Drip a little water on a clean area beside the stain. Beading points to a sealer or coating; if the concrete darkens within a minute, it is bare. Poultices and enzymes only work on bare, porous concrete, because both depend on oil moving through the pores. On a sealed floor the stain may be locked in or beneath the sealer, so strip that section before expecting any deep treatment to help.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stain profile</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Likely depth</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best first method</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical timeline</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dark, still slightly slick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shallow to moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Degreaser, then poultice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 days</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dull, dark, months old</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poultice, repeated</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-7 days</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Faded halo, years old</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Deep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Enzyme cleaner, kept damp</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 weeks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under a sealer or coating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">In or below film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip the area first</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gummy residue on top</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Surface plus depth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Scrape, hot water, then poultice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-5 days</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Will you coat the floor afterward?</h3>

          <p>
            Last, decide what the floor is for once you finish. Planning an epoxy coating raises the bar sharply, because coatings fail over oil contamination and often lift at that exact spot within a year. Coating makers commonly call for a water-drop test and a clean, degreased surface ahead of etching or grinding. If looks are all you care about, a stain faded to a light shadow may be acceptable, and a penetrating sealer will stop it from getting worse.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="poultice" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Do You Make a Poultice That Pulls Out Old Oil?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mix an absorbent powder with an oil-dissolving liquid, spread it over the stain and let it dry slowly under plastic. A poultice works as its liquid evaporates upward, carrying dissolved oil out of the pores and leaving it behind in the powder. Stone restorers have relied on the technique for marble and granite for decades, and the same physics applies to concrete.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What goes in the paste?</h3>

          <p>
            For the powder, use diatomaceous earth, clay kitty litter ground fine, powdered whiting, or all-purpose flour if nothing else is around. Diatomaceous earth is the most effective of the common options thanks to its extreme porosity. The liquid sets the chemistry: a concentrated water-based degreaser suits most stains, while acetone or mineral spirits can tackle very heavy oil on bare concrete, in a well-ventilated space only. Stir until it resembles peanut butter, stiff enough to stay put without running.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Are premixed poultices worth the money?</h3>

          <p>
            Ready-made products exist too. Pour-N-Restore is a liquid you pour on and brush in; it dries to a powder that holds the oil, in effect a poultice in a bottle. Several stone-care brands sell poultice powders that you mix with water or a solvent. Expect to pay more than for a homemade batch, roughly $15 to $30 for a bottle that treats a few stains at the time of writing, in exchange for a consistency you never have to guess at.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you apply it?</h3>

          <p>
            Begin with a clean, dry stain. Dampen the concrete lightly with the same liquid that went into the poultice, so the pores are primed to move oil. Spread the paste about a quarter inch thick and carry it an inch past the visible edge, because the oil has spread farther than you can see. Cover it with plastic sheeting, tape down the edges, and poke a few small holes in the plastic. Slow drying gives the paste more time to draw oil.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How many rounds does it take?</h3>

          <p>
            After 24 to 48 hours, peel off the plastic and let the poultice dry out completely. Lift it with a plastic putty knife, sweep up the residue, and dispose of it as oily waste. Rinse, give the slab a day to dry, then look. A moderate stain often loses half its darkness or more on the first pass. Keep repeating for as long as it keeps improving; three to five rounds is common for a garage floor stain built up over years.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SOLVENT SAFETY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Acetone and mineral spirits are flammable.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Mixing a solvent poultice? Raise the garage door first, know where the water heater, furnace and any pilot lights are, and keep every solvent well away from an ignition source. Put on chemical-resistant gloves and eye protection before you open the can. A poultice made with water-based degreaser works more slowly but takes the fire risk off the table entirely.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="enzymes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Stain Years Old and Spread Wide? Do Enzyme Cleaners Work?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, given time and moisture. Enzyme and microbial oil removers do not pull oil out at all; they deliver hydrocarbon-eating microbes plus the enzymes those microbes make. Terminator-HSD, sold as a powder you mix with water, is one example, alongside several liquid concentrates aimed at garage and shop floors. Over days or weeks the microbes break oil down into carbon dioxide and water, so they keep working inside the pores long after the surface looks clean.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you apply an enzyme cleaner?</h3>

          <p>
            The steps are easy; the waiting is the hard part. Clean the surface first so the microbes are not competing with dirt. Mix and apply the product as the label directs, work it in with a stiff brush, and keep the area damp, because microbes need moisture to stay active. Mist the spot or cover it with plastic, especially indoors where the air is dry. Hold off on rinsing for at least as long as the label specifies, often a day or more. Owners commonly report a stain that looks unchanged for several days and then fades noticeably over the following week.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does cold weather stall it?</h3>

          <p>
            Microbial cleaners slow sharply in the cold, and most labels suggest working when the concrete is above roughly 50 degrees Fahrenheit, so in an unheated garage in winter a poultice is the better bet. Keep bleach, acid and strong disinfectants off the area before and during treatment, since all of them kill the microbes. Rinse any earlier degreaser away thoroughly before the enzyme product goes down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When should you pick enzymes over a poultice?</h3>

          <p>
            Enzymes are at their best on large, faded stains that would eat up a lot of poultice material, and on surfaces where you would rather avoid solvents. They are also gentler around landscaping and pets than most alternatives. Speed is the price: if the floor has to be ready to coat by Saturday, begin with a poultice. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-oil-stain-remover-for-concrete/">best oil stain removers for concrete</a> ranks the products in this category.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Does Hot Water or a Pressure Washer Get Oil Out of Concrete?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heat helps a great deal; cold water on its own mostly does not. Oil thins as it warms, and thinner oil escapes concrete pores more easily, which is why commercial floor cleaning crews bring hot water pressure washers to fuel station aprons and shop floors. Paired with a degreaser, a hot water unit lifts far more oil per pass than cold water can. Most homeowner machines are cold water only, but there are other ways to add heat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you add heat without a hot water washer?</h3>

          <p>
            One option is to pour kettle-hot water over the stain, apply degreaser while the concrete is still warm, then scrub and rinse. Another is to work on a sunny afternoon once the slab has heated up, misting the cleaner so it never dries out. Some people warm a stain with a heat gun on a moderate setting before laying on a poultice. Keep the gun moving and never overheat one spot, because rapid heating can make the concrete surface spall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What can a cold water pressure washer do?</h3>

          <p>
            A cold water pressure wash rarely removes old oil by itself, but it makes an excellent rinse after a degreaser or a poultice, flushing emulsified oil out of the surface texture. Fit a 15-degree or 25-degree tip and hold it 8 to 12 inches from the concrete. On a big area, a rotating surface cleaner attachment leaves a more even finish and avoids the stripes a hand wand tends to leave. Our guide to the <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">best surface cleaners for pressure washers</a> matches attachments to machines.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When does renting a hot water unit pay off?</h3>

          <p>
            For a garage floor with years of buildup, renting a hot water pressure washer from an equipment rental shop for a day can be the most efficient route, at roughly $100 to $200 at the time of writing. Combined with a degreaser and a good wet vacuum to collect the wash water, it treats the entire floor in one session rather than chasing stains one at a time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="garage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Whole Garage Floor Stained? What Order Should You Work In?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Degrease the whole floor first, then treat the stains that survive. A garage floor rarely has a single stain; it usually has several of different ages plus general grime, and handling them in sequence saves time and material.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Day one: clear, scrape and degrease</h3>

          <p>
            Move everything off the floor and sweep well. Scrape thick, gummy deposits with a floor scraper or putty knife. Then spray a heavy alkaline degreaser over the entire floor with a pump sprayer, scrub it with a deck brush or floor machine, and pull the rinse water toward the door with a wet vacuum or squeegee.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Map what is left and treat it</h3>

          <p>
            Give the floor 24 to 48 hours to dry, then map the survivors, marking each one with tape or chalk. Fresh and medium stains get a poultice; large, faded patches get an enzyme treatment, and both can run at the same time on different spots. Cover the poultices, keep the enzyme areas damp, and stay off the floor for a couple of days. Then remove, rinse, dry and reassess.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Garage Floor Timeline at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Day 1</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sweep, scrape, degrease the whole floor</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Day 3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Mark stains, lay poultice or enzyme</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Day 5-7</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Remove, rinse, reassess, repeat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Week 2+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Seal or coat once fully dry</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How many rounds before you seal?</h3>

          <p>
            Keep repeating the targeted treatments until the improvement stops. On most garage floors, two rounds take care of the majority of stains and a third brings the rest down to faint shadows. Then choose your protection. A penetrating sealer keeps the bare concrete look and resists new stains; an epoxy or polyaspartic coating gives a fully sealed surface that wipes clean. If you go with a coating, follow the maker's prep, which usually means grinding or etching after degreasing, and see our picks for the <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">best garage floor epoxy</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where should the wash water go?</h3>

          <p>
            Not down the driveway. Degreasing runoff from a garage floor carries oil and should never flow into the storm drain. A wet vacuum makes collection simple. Let the collected water stand so the oil separates, then take the oily portion to your local household hazardous waste drop-off. Used poultice material and absorbent can usually go in household trash in small amounts, but check your local rules first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When is it worth calling a pro?</h3>

          <p>
            Hire out the job when it outgrows homeowner equipment: a large shop slab with years of buildup, a floor where an old sealer or coating has to come off across wide areas before any deep treatment can work, or a coating project whose prep calls for grinding. Commercial floor cleaning crews bring hot water pressure washers, the same machines used on fuel station aprons. Ask for a written quote that names the cleaning method and says whether the wash water will be collected rather than rinsed into the street.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="finish" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Shadow Still Showing? How Do You Know the Slab Is Ready to Seal?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Run a water test. Sprinkle water across the treated area and the concrete around it. Clean concrete soaks it up evenly and darkens. If water beads or sits on top over the old stain, oil is still near the surface, nothing will bond there, and that spot needs another round.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you paint over a leftover shadow?</h3>

          <p>
            Some owners try to hide a lingering shadow with concrete paint or a solid stain. On an otherwise clean slab that can work cosmetically, but paint over oil is a common cause of peeling, so the same water test applies first. For an even look without a coating, clean the whole floor with a <a href="/best-driveway-cleaner/">concrete and driveway cleaner</a>; that evens out the color so treated spots do not stand out as lighter patches.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which sealer slows the next stain?</h3>

          <p>
            For bare concrete you want to keep looking natural, a penetrating silane or siloxane sealer slows how quickly oil soaks in. It will not make the floor stainproof, but it buys time, often enough to blot a drip before it sets. Our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">best concrete sealers</a> separates the types that suit garage floors from those meant for exterior slabs. Reapply on the maker's schedule, usually every few years for penetrating products.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Have you fixed the leak?</h3>

          <p>
            Deal with the source last, and do not skip it. A leak that keeps dripping will undo any amount of cleaning. Park over a drip pan or containment mat until the car is repaired, and keep a bag of absorbent and a bottle of degreaser in the garage. Handling a drip within an hour takes a few minutes; handling it a year later takes a week of poultices.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes With Concrete Oil Stains
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting one scrub to fix a years-old stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Old oil lives well below the surface, so a single pass of degreaser cleans only the top. Many people quit after one try because the stain looks unchanged once the slab dries. Budget for several poultice rounds or a week or more of enzyme treatment, and measure progress by comparing the dry slab between rounds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the poultice dry too fast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A poultice only pulls oil while its liquid is evaporating upward. Left uncovered in sun or wind, it can dry within an hour and draw almost nothing out. Tape plastic over it, poke a few small holes, and leave it for a day or two before you scrape it off and judge the result.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Killing the microbes before they start</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Enzyme and microbial cleaners contain living organisms. Bleach, acid or a disinfecting cleaner used just beforehand will stop them, and so will letting the treated patch dry out. Rinse earlier chemicals away thoroughly, keep the area damp the whole time, and work within the temperature range on the label, typically concrete above roughly 50 degrees Fahrenheit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating over a stain that looks clean</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy and paint fail over oil even when a stain has faded to almost nothing. If water beads on the spot instead of soaking in, oil is still close to the surface. Keep treating until water absorbs evenly across the whole area, then follow the coating maker's etching or grinding instructions exactly as written.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using solvents in a closed garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acetone, mineral spirits and lacquer thinner give off flammable vapor that can drift to a water heater or furnace pilot. Always raise the garage door, ventilate well, and keep ignition sources off or far away, with gloves and eye protection on. If airflow is poor, switch to a water-based degreaser poultice or an enzyme product.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you get oil completely out of concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, if the stain is recent. Oil that has sat for a few months often clears completely after repeated poultices or an enzyme treatment. Stains soaked into a slab for many years can leave a faint shadow even after careful work, since part of the oil sits deeper than any method reaches. For those, aim for a spot that blends in and no longer feels slick.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best powder for a concrete oil poultice?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Diatomaceous earth is the strongest common pick, because its extreme porosity lets it hold a lot of oil. Clay kitty litter ground fine and powdered whiting cost less and also work well, while flour or cornstarch will do in a pinch. Blend the powder with a degreaser or solvent until it forms a thick paste that holds its place without running.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should an enzyme cleaner stay on concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The label has the final word, but most products need a day or more without rinsing and keep working for a week or longer while the area stays damp. Cold slows the microbes, so expect faster results once the concrete is above roughly 50 degrees Fahrenheit. Keep bleach and acid away from the area while the treatment works.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Coca-Cola or WD-40 remove oil from concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on set-in stains. The mild acidity in cola may loosen a little surface grime, but it does not dissolve oil and it leaves a sticky film behind. WD-40 can free up fresh grease, yet it also puts more petroleum into the slab. On old stains, a degreaser poultice or an enzyme cleaner accomplishes far more than either one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a garage floor coating cover oil stains?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only for a while. Coatings bond poorly to concrete contaminated with oil and commonly bubble or lift over those spots. Coating makers specify a clean, degreased surface before you etch or grind. Keep treating each stain until water soaks into it evenly, then prepare the whole floor exactly as the coating instructions describe.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a hot water pressure washer worth renting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, for a whole garage floor or a badly stained shop slab. Heated water thins oil so it lets go of the pores much more readily than it does under cold water. At the time of writing, rental shops typically charge roughly $100 to $200 a day. If you only have one or two spots, a poultice costs less and works just as well.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop oil from staining the floor again?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Repair the leak, park over a drip pan or containment mat until then, and seal the slab. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer slows absorption enough that you can blot a spill before it sets, while an epoxy or polyaspartic coating gives a surface that wipes clean. Keep absorbent and degreaser on hand so drips get treated within the hour.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

