        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Old oil in concrete is a problem of depth, not effort. Clean the surface first so you can see what is really left, then choose a method that reaches beneath it: a covered poultice for stains you want gone this week, an enzyme cleaner for big, faded patches you can leave alone for a while, and heat to speed up either one.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Judge each round on dry concrete, keep going while the stain keeps fading, and stop once the spot no longer feels slick and water soaks in evenly. Then seal or coat the slab and repair the leak, so the work you just finished holds up.
          </p>
        </div>

